{{Economy of Balisca}}
The '''Tricontinental Company''' ({{lang-bl|Compañía Tricontinental}}) is a [[Balisca]]n {{W|Freight transport|shipping}} company, active in ocean and inland freight transportation and associated services, such as {{W|supply chain management}} and {{W|port operator|port operation}}.

== History ==

=== 1865-1900 ===
The company traces its history back to the ''Trans-Etiopic'' shipping company founded in 1865. The company inaugurated Balisca's first [[W:Passenger liner|passenger liner]] service, with a route from [[Ampuria]] to [[Alessandria]]; in that same year, the company name was changed to Trans-Aethiopic Mail Steamship Company. 

The merged company had a fleet of 58 [[W:Steamship|steamships]] and expanded its operations rapidly, first to other Latin American ports and then worldwide, with a line service to [[Kotabumi]] established in 1888 and to [[W:London|London]] in 1889.

The company's ''[[SS Hesperia]]'' was used by [[W:Islam in China|Chinese Muslims]] to travel to Singapore on their way to [[W:Makkah|Makkah]] for the [[W:Hajj|Hajj]] in 1925. From there, the company had the pilgrims travel on board other Baliscan steamships to Suez and then to Makkah. The company promised to take responsibility for all the necessary formalities and helped contact other local transportation agencies that could take the pilgrims to Makkah. Chinese pilgrims were promised a 20% discount for their tickets. A third-class ticket that sold for £5/10/0 would be £4/8/0, while a second-class ticket sold for £14/0/0 would be sold for £11/5/0.

=== 1900-1945 ===
The majority of Baliscan merchant ships, tankers, and liners sailed under the Tricontinental banner in this period. Regular services linked [[Alessandria]] and [[Concepción]] with [[Sanyan]], [[W:Lagos|Lagos]], [[W:Jakarta|Batavia]], [[W:Melbourne|Melbourne]], and [[W:Cape Town|Cape Town]], with frequent crossings to [[W:Barcelona|Barcelona]] and [[Lisbon]]. Other routes connected local Baliscan [[W:Cabotage|cabotage]] vessels on the South American coasts and upper [[W:Rio de la Plata|Rio de la Plata]].

The busiest routes were from the east, making stops in [[Kotabumi]] (Kartasugih) or Cape Town. These routes connected Balisca with [[W:South Asia|South]] and [[W:Southeast Asia|Southeast Asia]] and the Levant through the [[W:Suez Canal|Suez Canal]],  and also included the China coasts, and India and the Indian Ocean. 

Local sea routes connected 78 home seaports (38 open to foreign trade). Alessandria, Concepción, [[Macaio]] and [[Aigües]] had the greatest importance for trading with Balisca. These ports had the third, fourth, and eighth place in net tonnage registered in the world. Importantly, coal passed from [[Calatañazor]] to Alessandria and Concepción, while hemp, cotton, salt, and minerals represented other important parts of these transport transactions. 

From 1924, all new cargo ships for Tricontinental were [[W:Motor ship|motor ships]]. Trincontinental introduced its first passenger motor ships in 1929, but continued to buy a mixture of steam and motor passenger ships until 1939.

In World War II, Tricontinental provided military transport and [[W:Hospital ship|hospital ships]] for the [[Baliscan Expeditionary Force]] and the [[Confederal Navy]]. Many vessels were lost to German [[W:U-boat|U-boats]].

